# No look-dev panels

Needle is available for live scene edits. Do not add a Tweakpane, lil-gui, dat.GUI, or custom debug panel just to expose appearance and scene-look controls. Do not add the dependency, DOM node, or CSS for that panel either.

Write the value directly in code. When the user asks to try a color, metalness, roughness, or similar detail on a running page, change it with Needle (`inspector_property_write` and related inspector tools) instead of building UI.

## Do not add controls for

- Colors: material color, gradient stops, background, light color
- Surface: metalness, roughness, transmission, IOR, clearcoat, sheen, emissive, opacity, wireframe
- Look-dev scene knobs: exposure, rotation speed, time scale, and light intensity or position sliders whose only job is visual tuning

```javascript
// Bad: a panel whose only job is tuning the look
pane.addBinding(params, 'metalness', { min: 0, max: 1 })

// Good: a fixed value on the material or uniform
material.metalness = 0.65
```

## Still add UI when

- The user explicitly asks for a panel, slider, or debug GUI
- The control is part of the experience itself (a game HUD, or a tool the end user must operate), not a stand-in for look-dev
